{"id":"g-apple-card-what","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"text","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":true,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":"Matches the reference \u2014 Chase will become the new issuer via a Dec 30, 2025 forward purchase commitment to acquire the Apple credit card portfolio with closing in ~24 months; candidate adds consistent exposure estimates.","answer":"On January 7, 2026, JPMorganChase announced that Chase will become the new issuer of Apple Card. The Firm had entered a forward purchase commitment on December 30, 2025 to acquire the Apple credit card portfolio, with an expected closing in approximately 24 months \u2014 this is referred to as the \u0022Apple Card transaction.\u0022 Estimated total credit exposure at closing is approximately $104\u2013106 billion, including roughly $23\u201324 billion of estimated drawn loans [1][2][4][7][8].","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":24702,"outputTokens":253,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":8400,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":true,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["de4a4a76-f1f5-4a12-8e19-c5f208313058","4594ae18-35e0-4227-87a1-fed45756ded3","11e0eedd-8ed0-4061-b543-a2c33516cd3d","262bd853-90c9-4d46-b4fc-d4b548bb8603","5ea16272-ffa7-416e-af65-e0531d473005","d386dc2d-89f4-439b-8097-60272c69ecd0","f43c291a-46e7-4d3a-ad1e-013cac8e5b1f","e8f132f8-eb41-42ce-8f27-5ac9656ad856","ef4d1724-30d1-4101-a0df-a444456f139d","a97d4268-4cee-4d48-a7fa-7bd96796be06"],"turns":2,"agentCostUsd":0.047492400000000004}
{"id":"g-apple-card-allowance","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"number","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":true,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":null,"answer":"The Apple Card transaction added **$2.2 billion** to JPMorgan Chase\u0027s allowance for credit losses in 2025, recorded in Q4 2025 within the consumer portfolio segment [1][3][9][10].","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":25296,"outputTokens":178,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":6716,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":true,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["f8adbcb7-27fb-40f3-933f-b7918102e2d9","ce120d6a-0a4b-4c07-80a3-eafa892f8e44","945b78aa-f4a8-419b-a0fa-c7c6560f740f","641336df-3081-4bd0-9e01-456711e99a1b","4594ae18-35e0-4227-87a1-fed45756ded3","ffceffe8-fe8f-4965-856c-90cb06867154","e8f132f8-eb41-42ce-8f27-5ac9656ad856","005e9cc3-6b82-4666-acad-c4f76b6750c4","f49fff94-23c2-4f57-aca2-223f2f8101b7","8492e4dc-fdf6-4cd9-83fb-5901f3a27e1e"],"turns":2,"agentCostUsd":0.0491074}
{"id":"g-expense-why","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"text","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":true,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":"Lists the same key reasons\u2014mainly higher compensation (headcount and revenue-related pay) and increases in brokerage/distribution fees, auto-lease depreciation, technology and marketing investments, and occupancy expense.","answer":"JPMorgan Chase\u0027s total noninterest expense rose to $95.6 billion in 2025, up 4% from $91.8 billion in 2024 [1][3]. The increase was driven by:\n\n- **Higher compensation expense** ($54.5B vs $51.4B) \u2014 from growth in headcount (especially front-office employees) and higher revenue-related compensation, particularly in CIB and AWM [1][2][3]\n- **Higher noncompensation expense**, including:\n  - Higher brokerage expense (CIB) and distribution fees (AWM) [2]\n  - Higher auto lease depreciation on larger auto operating lease assets (CCB) [2][3]\n  - Continued investments in technology (up to $11.0B from $9.8B) and marketing (up to $5.5B from $5.0B) [1][2][3]\n  - Higher occupancy expense ($5.5B vs $5.0B), reflecting property additions/improvements including the new headquarters, bank branches, and other corporate offices [1][2]\n\nThese increases were partially offset by FDIC special assessment accrual releases of $763 million (vs. a $725 million accrual increase in 2024) and the absence of a $1.0 billion Visa shares contribution to the JPMorgan Chase Foundation recorded in Q2 2024 [8].","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":24890,"outputTokens":534,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":11689,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":true,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["02bf08f3-cae5-472a-80d6-f3f9dc4cfae4","7a42b421-6450-43e5-956f-a7b8b28bc4cc","9c9192d3-6668-48f3-8c61-918db7d000e3","c65ef18d-f436-4cda-81a7-ac5025869a9a","ca169d6b-81e7-4046-9990-9079f8c02e82","004d4d92-9947-438d-8b5d-2b3a1ee603ee","cd695826-c3a2-49d1-8a01-0e1e891fa659","472bfae0-81d4-41e3-b931-73d2d2dfabae","788d4432-fb8a-49fa-b8c2-a0984e2cc1d9","b28ba6e3-22b6-4283-96d0-59a980cab594"],"turns":2,"agentCostUsd":0.0510694}
